# The commit whose guest runtime image the production metal fleet is running.
#
# Read by the `dispatch-metal-agent-release` gate in deploy.yml to decide whether
# a production release must ask hosts to rebuild their golden rootfs. The rebuild
# re-stamps the rootfs identity, which invalidates EVERY snapshot on every host —
# ~6,500 fleet-wide — so each project cold-boots on its next open instead of
# resuming. Worth it when guests have new code to run; a self-inflicted outage
# when they do not. On 2026-08-09 an unconditional rebuild cost a day of 524s for
# a release that changed nothing inside the guest.
#
# The tag history cannot answer this on its own. A guest image can reach the
# fleet outside a tagged release — 8b4232bb did, hand-rolled that afternoon to
# ship the idle-reaper fix — after which "changed since the last tag" reports
# work the fleet has already done.
#
# UPDATE THIS ONLY AFTER THE FLEET HAS ACTUALLY REBUILT ON THE NEW IMAGE.
# Getting it wrong in that direction is the harmful one: a baseline ahead of the
# fleet silently skips rebuilds and leaves guests on stale code indefinitely,
# which is how a runtime bug survives weeks of releases. A baseline left behind
# the fleet only costs an unnecessary rebuild, which is why that is the default
# whenever this file is missing or unreadable.
#
# Verify before changing it, don't assume. The image is greppable:
#   ssh root@<host> "grep -ac -m1 <symbol-from-the-change> /opt/fc-spike/img/runtime.ext4"
#
# be0c37c (v1.13.26) verified on all four production hosts 2026-08-13: ROOTFS_SHA
# reads be0c37c and the image contains `reportErrorToParent`, the symbol d6be7e2e
# added to canvas-bridge.js — a commit that is inside v1.13.26 and absent from the
# previous baseline. So the fleet has rebuilt, and leaving the baseline behind it
# would have had the next release rebuild for a guest change already shipped.
#
# c09700b (v2.0.0) verified on all four production hosts 2026-09-23, but only
# after finding the ROOTFS_SHA marker itself is not trustworthy: the 2026-09-23
# release raced its rootfs-rebuild dispatch against the runtime-multiarch image
# publish — hosts pulled `production-multiarch-latest` and rebuilt at 09:18 UTC,
# 22 minutes BEFORE the real c09700b image finished pushing to that tag at
# 09:40:23 UTC, so all four hosts baked in the stale prior image (dated 2026-09-18)
# while writeRootfsMarker() stamped ROOTFS_SHA=c09700b anyway — silently blocking
# the correctly-timed second dispatch via the version-based dedup in
# shouldRebuildRootfs(). Symbol grep for `getRootPreviewManager`/`getAnchorProjectId`
# (added in ac4ea2c75, inside v2.0.0, absent from the prior baseline) was 0 on all
# four hosts despite ROOTFS_SHA already reading c09700b — a real fleet-wide gap of
# ~191 commits behind the reported version. Fixed by manually re-pulling the (by
# then genuinely published) image and clearing each host's ROOTFS_SHA marker so
# the agent's own heartbeat re-triggered a real rebuild; re-verified afterward —
# all four hosts now show 10/5 matches for those symbols. The image-publish vs.
# rebuild-dispatch race itself is still unfixed in the pipeline; see the
# 2026-09-23 investigation for the follow-up.

